{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d3_react_react-v18","statement":"React 18 was announced as now available on npm.","source_span":"React 18 is now available on npm!"}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d3_react_react-v18","statement":"The React v18.0 announcement post was published March 29, 2022 by The React Team.","source_span":"March 29, 2022 by [The React Team](/community/team)"}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d3_react_react-v18","statement":"React 18 includes automatic batching, the new startTransition API, and streaming server-side rendering with Suspense support.","source_span":"automatic batching, new APIs like startTransition, and streaming server-side rendering with support for Suspense"}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d3_react_react-v18","statement":"Many React 18 features are built on top of a new concurrent renderer, and Concurrent React is opt-in, enabled only when a concurrent feature is used.","source_span":"Concurrent React is opt-in — it's only enabled when you use a concurrent feature"}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d3_react_react-v18","statement":"The React team formed the React 18 Working Group in the summer of 2021 to gather community feedback ahead of the release.","source_span":"Last summer, we formed the React 18 Working Group to gather feedback from experts in the community"}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d3_react_react-v18","statement":"React 18 will ship in React Native together with the New React Native Architecture.","source_span":"React 18 will ship in React Native with the New React Native Architecture"}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d3_react_react-v18","statement":"In a concurrent render, React may start rendering an update, pause in the middle, continue later, or abandon the render entirely, while guaranteeing the UI stays consistent.","source_span":"React may start rendering an update, pause in the middle, then continue later. It may even abandon an in-progress render altogether. React guarantees that the UI will appear consistent even if a render is interrupted."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d3_react_react-v18","statement":"React plans to add a new component called <Offscreen> to let UI be removed from the screen and later restored while reusing its previous state.","source_span":"we're planning to add a new component called `<Offscreen>` that implements this pattern"}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d3_react_react-v18","statement":"StrictMode helps surface concurrency-related bugs during development by logging extra warnings and double-invoking functions expected to be idempotent, without affecting production behavior.","source_span":"Strict Mode doesn't affect production behavior, but during development it will log extra warnings and double-invoke functions that are expected to be idempotent"}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d3_react_react-v18","statement":"At React Conf 2021, Shaundai Person gave an overview of streaming server rendering with Suspense.","source_span":"[Shaundai Person](https://twitter.com/shaundai) gave us an overview of [streaming server rendering with Suspense]"}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d3_react_react-v18","fact_refs":[0,1],"question":"When was the React v18.0 release blog post published and who authored it?","answer":"It was published on March 29, 2022 by The React Team."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d3_react_react-v18","fact_refs":[2],"question":"What are three out-of-the-box improvements included in React 18?","answer":"React 18 includes automatic batching, the new startTransition API, and streaming server-side rendering with Suspense support."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d3_react_react-v18","fact_refs":[3],"question":"Is Concurrent React enabled by default in React 18, or does a developer need to opt in?","answer":"Concurrent React is opt-in — it is only enabled when the app uses a concurrent feature."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d3_react_react-v18","fact_refs":[4],"question":"When did the React team form the React 18 Working Group, and why?","answer":"They formed it in the summer of 2021 to gather feedback from community experts and ensure a smooth upgrade experience for the React ecosystem."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d3_react_react-v18","fact_refs":[5],"question":"How will React 18 be delivered to React Native users?","answer":"React 18 will ship in React Native together with the New React Native Architecture."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d3_react_react-v18","fact_refs":[6],"question":"What can happen to an update's render in Concurrent React, and does React still guarantee UI consistency?","answer":"React may start rendering an update, pause partway through, resume later, or abandon it altogether — but it still guarantees the UI appears consistent, since it defers DOM mutations until the whole tree is evaluated."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d3_react_react-v18","fact_refs":[7],"question":"What new component was React planning to add to support reusable state when hiding and showing UI?","answer":"A new component called `<Offscreen>`, which lets a section of UI be removed from the screen and later restored while reusing its previous state."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d3_react_react-v18","fact_refs":[8],"question":"How does StrictMode help with concurrency bugs, and does it affect production behavior?","answer":"StrictMode logs extra warnings and double-invokes functions expected to be idempotent during development to surface concurrency-related bugs; it does not affect production behavior."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d3_react_react-v18","fact_refs":[9],"question":"Who presented on streaming server rendering with Suspense at React Conf 2021?","answer":"Shaundai Person gave an overview of streaming server rendering with Suspense."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d3_react_react-labs-what-we-have-been-working-on-june-2022","statement":"The React Labs post was published June 15, 2022 by a group of authors including Andrew Clark, Dan Abramov, Jan Kassens, Joseph Savona, Josh Story, Lauren Tan, Luna Ruan, Mengdi Chen, Rick Hanlon, Robert Zhang, Sathya Gunasekaran, Sebastian Markbage, and Xuan Huang.","source_span":"June 15, 2022 by [Andrew Clark]...and [Xuan Huang]"}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d3_react_react-labs-what-we-have-been-working-on-june-2022","statement":"The React team announced an experimental demo of React Server Components (RSC) in December 2020.","source_span":"We announced an experimental demo of React Server Components (RSC) in December 2020."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d3_react_react-labs-what-we-have-been-working-on-june-2022","statement":"The React team is abandoning forked I/O libraries (e.g. react-fetch) for RSC in favor of an async/await model for better compatibility.","source_span":"we're abandoning the idea of having forked I/O libraries (eg react-fetch), and instead adopting an async/await model for better compatibility"}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d3_react_react-labs-what-we-have-been-working-on-june-2022","statement":"The React team is working with Vercel and Shopify to unify bundler support for shared RSC semantics in both webpack and Vite, which is described as the major blocker to reaching stable.","source_span":"We're working together with Vercel and Shopify to unify bundler support for shared semantics in both webpack and Vite...This is the major blocker for reaching stable."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d3_react_react-labs-what-we-have-been-working-on-june-2022","statement":"React gave an early preview of the React Forget compiler at React Conf 2021, which automatically generates the equivalent of useMemo and useCallback calls to minimize re-render cost.","source_span":"We gave an early preview of React Forget at React Conf 2021. It's a compiler that automatically generates the equivalent of `useMemo` and `useCallback` calls to minimize the cost of re-rendering"}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d3_react_react-labs-what-we-have-been-working-on-june-2022","statement":"The React compiler was rewritten with a new architecture that can analyze and memoize more complex patterns such as local mutations.","source_span":"we finished a rewrite of the compiler to make it more reliable and capable. This new architecture allows us to analyze and memoize more complex patterns such as the use of local mutations"}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d3_react_react-labs-what-we-have-been-working-on-june-2022","statement":"Offscreen introduces a third option for hiding/showing components: hide the UI visually but deprioritize its rendering work, unlike unmounting (loses state) or CSS toggling (keeps rendering at a performance cost).","source_span":"Offscreen introduces a third option: hide the UI visually, but deprioritize its content."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d3_react_react-labs-what-we-have-been-working-on-june-2022","fact_refs":[0],"question":"When was the 'React Labs: What We've Been Working On – June 2022' post published?","answer":"It was published on June 15, 2022."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d3_react_react-labs-what-we-have-been-working-on-june-2022","fact_refs":[1],"question":"When did React first announce an experimental demo of React Server Components?","answer":"In December 2020."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d3_react_react-labs-what-we-have-been-working-on-june-2022","fact_refs":[2],"question":"What approach is the React team adopting instead of forked I/O libraries like react-fetch for Server Components?","answer":"They are adopting an async/await model for better compatibility, instead of forked I/O libraries such as react-fetch."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d3_react_react-labs-what-we-have-been-working-on-june-2022","fact_refs":[3],"question":"Which companies is the React team working with to unify bundler support for React Server Components, and across which bundlers?","answer":"Vercel and Shopify, to unify shared RSC semantics across both webpack and Vite — described as the major blocker to reaching a stable release."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d3_react_react-labs-what-we-have-been-working-on-june-2022","fact_refs":[4],"question":"What is React Forget and when was it first previewed?","answer":"React Forget is a compiler that automatically generates the equivalent of useMemo and useCallback calls to minimize re-rendering costs; it was first previewed at React Conf 2021."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d3_react_react-labs-what-we-have-been-working-on-june-2022","fact_refs":[5],"question":"What new capability did rewriting the React compiler's architecture unlock?","answer":"The rewritten architecture allows the compiler to analyze and memoize more complex patterns, such as the use of local mutations, beyond what was possible with manual memoization Hooks."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d3_react_react-labs-what-we-have-been-working-on-june-2022","fact_refs":[6],"question":"How does the proposed Offscreen feature differ from unmounting or CSS-based hiding of a component?","answer":"Offscreen hides the UI visually while deprioritizing its rendering work, so React can defer updates until idle — unlike unmounting (which loses state) or CSS toggling (which keeps rendering the hidden component at a performance cost)."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d3_react_introducing-react-dev","statement":"react.dev, the new home for React and its documentation, launched March 16, 2023.","source_span":"March 16, 2023 by [Dan Abramov]...Today we are thrilled to launch [react.dev]"}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d3_react_introducing-react-dev","statement":"The introducing react.dev post was authored by Dan Abramov and Rachel Nabors.","source_span":"author: Dan Abramov and Rachel Nabors"}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d3_react_introducing-react-dev","statement":"The new React site includes diagrams, illustrations, challenges, and over 600 new interactive examples.","source_span":"We've included diagrams, illustrations, challenges, and over 600 new interactive examples."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d3_react_introducing-react-dev","statement":"The previous React documentation site moved to legacy.reactjs.org, and the old reactjs.org domain now redirects to react.dev.","source_span":"The previous React documentation site has now moved to [legacy.reactjs.org]...The old [reactjs.org] domain will now redirect here."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d3_react_introducing-react-dev","statement":"The new docs are divided into two main sections: Learn React, a self-paced course, and API Reference, which documents every React API.","source_span":"**[Learn React](/learn)** is a self-paced course that teaches React from scratch...**[API Reference](/reference)** provides the details and usage examples for every React API."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d3_react_introducing-react-dev","statement":"React Hooks were released in 2018, and the original Hooks docs assumed readers were already familiar with class components.","source_span":"When we released React Hooks in 2018, the Hooks docs assumed the reader is familiar with class components."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d3_react_introducing-react-dev","statement":"The Learn section begins with the Quick Start page, and the recommended next step for learning by doing is the Tic-Tac-Toe Tutorial.","source_span":"The Learn section begins with the [Quick Start](/learn) page...we recommend checking out the [Tic-Tac-Toe Tutorial](/learn/tutorial-tic-tac-toe) next"}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d3_react_introducing-react-dev","fact_refs":[0,1],"question":"When did react.dev launch and who wrote the announcement post?","answer":"react.dev launched on March 16, 2023, announced in a post by Dan Abramov and Rachel Nabors."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d3_react_introducing-react-dev","fact_refs":[2],"question":"How many new interactive examples were added to the new React documentation site?","answer":"Over 600 new interactive examples were added, along with diagrams, illustrations, and challenges."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d3_react_introducing-react-dev","fact_refs":[3],"question":"What happened to the old React documentation site and the reactjs.org domain after react.dev launched?","answer":"The old documentation moved to legacy.reactjs.org, and the old reactjs.org domain now redirects to react.dev."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d3_react_introducing-react-dev","fact_refs":[4],"question":"What are the two main sections of the new react.dev documentation?","answer":"Learn React, a self-paced course teaching React from scratch, and API Reference, which provides details and usage examples for every React API."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d3_react_introducing-react-dev","fact_refs":[5],"question":"When were React Hooks released, and what assumption did the original Hooks docs make about readers?","answer":"React Hooks were released in 2018, and the original docs assumed readers were already familiar with class components."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d3_react_introducing-react-dev","fact_refs":[6],"question":"What page does the Learn section start with, and what tutorial is recommended next for learning by doing?","answer":"It starts with the Quick Start page, and the Tic-Tac-Toe Tutorial is recommended next for those who like to learn by doing."}
